I know this is convoluted - I tried to make my data into a move object directly from .csv using:
datam<-move(data$Long.1,data$Lat.1,time = as.POSIXct(paste(data$Date,data$Time),format="%d/%m/%Y %H:%M",tz="GMT"),proj = CRS("+proj=tmerc +lat_0=49 +lon_0=-2 +k=0.9996012717 +x_0=400000 +y_0=-100000 +datum=OSGB36 +units=m +no_defs +ellps=airy +towgs84=446.448,-125.157,542.060,0.1502,0.2470,0.8421,-20.4894"),sensor="GPS",animal=data$ID)

here received the 'timestamps are not ascending' error  - but i am unable to find where or why this is happening. I have tried 'data <- data[order(data$ID),]' but i still get the same or similar ('Error in validityMethod(as(object, superClass)) : Not ordered timestamps per individual occured').

When i transform to ltraj object, then to move (which works) and THEN to as.telemetry using:
trj<-as.ltraj(coordinates(undupl),date = undupl$da,id = undupl$ID, burst = undupl$ID,typeII = TRUE)
datam<-move(trj)
telem<-as.telemetry.Move(datam,timeformat = "%d/%m/%Y %H:%M",timezone = "GMT",projection = CRS("+proj=tmerc +lat_0=49 +lon_0=-2 +k=0.9996012717 +x_0=400000 +y_0=-100000 +datum=OSGB36 +units=m +no_defs +ellps=airy +towgs84=446.448,-125.157,542.060,0.1502,0.2470,0.8421,-20.4894"),timeout = max,na.rm="row",mark.rm = FALSE,drop = FALSE)

I get:

#Error in timeout < Inf :
# comparison (3) is possible only for atomic and list types
and also an error of:
#Error in spTransform(xSP, CRSobj, ...) :
# No transformation possible from NA reference system

My date/time data was transformed to as.POSIXct format early on when i made the SPDF and were allocated to GMT and British National Grid format, so i am confused why it won't work... where is the most obvious place that the issue could lie? I assume that there is some issue with the timestamps that I can't see? Is my data file able to be made into as.telemetry? I assume so but i was wondering if I'm missing something....

Thanks in advance for any help you can provide...

Lucy

On Friday, 15 February 2019 03:01:21 UTC, Christen Fleming wrote:
> Hi Steve,
>
> This sounds like problems with the timestamp format or timezone. I can point out a couple of things:
>
> You could be feeding in different timestamps into move() and as.telemetry(). For move() you did not supply a timezone argument in as.POSIXct(), which means that the (default) local timezone of your computer was used. Yet for as.telemetry(), you supplied the timezone of "America/Los_Angeles". I would also note, that most collars/tags are UTC, to avoid timezone issues, and not the timezone of the study area.
> When you open and re-save a CSV file in Excel, it can reformat your timestamps. Usually this is a danger because it can corrupt your data, but in this case it could have served to fix or better standardize your timestamps.
> If that doesn't fix your problems, then you can feel free to send me some sample data to replicate the issue and I can take a look.
>
> Best,
> Chris
>
